public without sharing class AccountRatingService {
    @future
    public static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Map<Id, String> desiredRatings = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Id accountId : accountIds) {
            desiredRatings.put(accountId, 'Cold');
        }

        for (AggregateResult result : [
            SELECT AccountId, MAX(Amount) maxAmount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
                AND IsClosed = false
            GROUP BY AccountId
        ]) {
            Id accountId = (Id) result.get('AccountId');
            Decimal maxAmount = (Decimal) result.get('maxAmount');

            desiredRatings.put(
                accountId,
                maxAmount != null && maxAmount >= 100000 ? 'Hot' : 'Warm'
            );
        }

        List<Account> accountsToUpdate = new List<Account>();
        for (Account account : [
            SELECT Id, Rating
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
        ]) {
            String desiredRating = desiredRatings.get(account.Id);
            if (account.Rating != desiredRating) {
                accountsToUpdate.add(new Account(
                    Id = account.Id,
                    Rating = desiredRating
                ));
            }
        }

        if (!accountsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update accountsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

I use one future call per trigger execution with a set of account IDs, rather than one call per opportunity. Salesforce limits future calls per transaction, so a call per record can fail for a 200-record batch. I also recalculate both the old and new accounts when an opportunity is reassigned, so neither account’s rating is left stale.

