public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> matches = new List<Id>();

        if (leads == null) {
            return matches;
        }

        // Index contacts once, keeping the first contact for each normalized email.
        // This avoids up to 9 million comparisons for the stated input sizes.
        Map<String, Id> contactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact contactRecord : contacts) {
                if (contactRecord == null || String.isBlank(contactRecord.Email)) {
                    continue;
                }

                String normalizedEmail =
                    contactRecord.Email.trim().toLowerCase();

                if (!contactIdByEmail.containsKey(normalizedEmail)) {
                    contactIdByEmail.put(normalizedEmail, contactRecord.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        for (Lead leadRecord : leads) {
            Id matchedContactId;
            if (leadRecord != null && !String.isBlank(leadRecord.Email)) {
                String normalizedEmail = leadRecord.Email.trim().toLowerCase();
                if (contactIdByEmail.containsKey(normalizedEmail)) {
                    matchedContactId = contactIdByEmail.get(normalizedEmail);
                }
            }
            matches.add(matchedContactId);
        }

        return matches;
    }
}
```

A trigger transaction can enqueue at most 50 Queueable jobs. Enqueuing one job per case would therefore fail when a transaction closes 200 cases. The trigger below enqueues one starter job; the Queueable processes one case per job and chains the next, so each case is still handled in its own asynchronous transaction. Task insert failures are logged without stopping the chain.

